          "John of the Cross (1542–1591) wrote of a ‘dark night’ in which prayer that once felt sweet becomes dry. He taught that this can be God’s loving work, weaning us from needing spiritual pleasure so that we can love him for himself. One sign he gave is that the person still cares deeply about God, with “painful care and solicitude, thinking that it is not serving God”, which, he said, is not lukewarmness at all. But he also said dryness could come from illness or “melancholy” and must be distinguished; so this is not a reason to ignore depression.",
          "After Mother Teresa’s death, her letters were published as <em>Come Be My Light</em> (2007). They revealed that for some 45 years, almost all the time she led the Missionaries of Charity, she felt a painful sense of God’s absence, broken only by brief consolation. She wrote, “In my heart there is no faith—no love—no trust—there is so much pain—the pain of longing, the pain of not being wanted.” Her postulator, Fr Brian Kolodiejchuk, called it not a loss of faith but “a trial of faith”; she herself said, “I know this is only feelings.” Some commentators read the letters more darkly, as real doubt. Either way, she kept loving God and the poor without the felt reward. The Catholic Church later declared her a saint (2016)."
