  {
    id: "manifestations",
    q: "What about shaking, falling, laughter, “drunk in the Spirit”, trembling and other bodily manifestations in Scripture and revival history?",
    short: "Scripture shows people trembling, falling and overwhelmed in God’s presence, and revival history is full of such scenes. But bodily effects prove nothing either way: they can come from God, from ordinary human emotion and suggestion, or from unhealthy sources. Jonathan Edwards’s balanced test still helps: judge by the fruit and the focus on Christ, not by the body’s reactions.",
    sections: [
      {
        title: "What many of us were taught",
        body: [
          "Some were taught that any shaking, falling or laughing in church is fleshly, demonic or just theatre. Others were taught that these are the signs that God has really ‘shown up’, and that if you are not affected you are resisting the Spirit. Many have watched videos of the Toronto Blessing or similar meetings and not known what to think: some scenes look holy, some look odd, and some look like pressure.",
          "Both reactions can be unfair. The first can mock genuine people who were deeply moved by God. The second can push people to perform and make the quiet feel second class."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "Scripture in context, with the original words",
        body: [
          "The Bible does show bodies responding to God. Abraham “fell on his face” (Gen 17:3). When God’s glory (<em>kavod</em>, literally ‘weight’) filled the temple, the priests “could not stand to minister” (2 Chr 5:14). Jeremiah said, “all my bones shake; I am like a drunken man... because of the LORD” (Jer 23:9, KJV). Daniel lost his strength and trembled (Dan 10:8-11). Ezekiel fell on his face (Ezek 1:28); John “fell at his feet as dead” (Rev 1:17). Hebrew <em>charad</em>, ‘to tremble’, is used of those who “tremble at my word” (Isa 66:2). The place where the disciples prayed “was shaken” (Acts 4:31).",
          "At Pentecost, onlookers mocked, “These men are full of new wine”, and Peter replied, “these are not drunken, as ye suppose” (Acts 2:13, 15, KJV). Paul later contrasts being drunk with wine with being “filled with the Spirit” (Eph 5:18). The Greek for ‘be drunk’ is <em>methuskesthe</em>. Paul is not commanding a drunken state; he is saying the Spirit, not wine, should fill and govern us, shown in singing, thankfulness and mutual submission (5:19-21). The ‘drunk in the Spirit’ language is an image some draw from these texts; it is not a biblical command to lose control. Greek <em>ekstasis</em> appears seven times in the New Testament, mostly as ‘amazement’ (Mark 5:42; 16:8; Luke 5:26; Acts 3:10) and three times as ‘trance’ (Acts 10:10; 11:5; 22:17).",
          "Honesty matters here too. In John 18:6 the soldiers who came to arrest Jesus “went backward, and fell to the ground”; that was not a blessing. In Mark 9:20-26 a boy convulses because of a spirit, and Jesus sets him free. So the Bible shows that bodies can react strongly for different reasons. ‘Slain in the Spirit’ and ‘resting in the Spirit’ are modern phrases, not biblical terms."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "Revival history: Edwards, Cane Ridge, Azusa, Toronto",
        body: [
          "In the Great Awakening (1740s), people wept, cried out and fainted. Jonathan Edwards, a careful Reformed theologian, wrote that a work is not to be judged by “effects on the bodies of men; such as tears, trembling, groans, loud outcries, agonies of body, or the failing of bodily strength”: they do not prove it is God, nor prove it is not. He also urged people to restrain such outward manifestations as far as they could during solemn worship, and warned strongly against spiritual pride.",
          "At Cane Ridge, Kentucky (August 1801), estimates of attendance ran from 10,000 to 25,000. People fell, groaned, laughed, and some had ‘the jerks’. Only a minority fell, and some ministers worried that preachers were coaxing emotionalism. At Azusa Street, Los Angeles (1906–1909), William Seymour’s mission saw tongues, shouting, trances and holy dance, and a striking (if imperfect) crossing of racial lines. At the Toronto Airport Vineyard from January 1994, meetings were marked by crying, shaking, laughter and falling. In December 1995 John Wimber released the church from the Vineyard, unhappy with its emphasis on the manifestations. Many people testified to real blessing; critics pointed to excesses, and some odd behaviours were widely publicised.",
          "History, then, shows a mixed picture: genuine repentance, joy and lasting fruit, alongside mess, pressure and division. That is roughly what Edwards expected: “A work of God without stumbling-blocks is never to be expected.”"
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "A fair counter-view, and the relational response",
        body: [
          "Critics, including cessationists like John MacArthur, argue that these phenomena are largely the product of suggestion, crowd psychology and showmanship; that they distract from the preaching of the gospel; and that Scripture never tells us to seek them. They point to 1 Corinthians 14:40, “Let all things be done decently and in order”, and to the Spirit’s fruit of self-control. Some psychologists would add that falling and shaking are well-known responses to expectation, emotion and group settings.",
          "The relational gospel concedes a lot of this. Bodily effects are not proof of God, and they can be produced by people. Pressure to fall or shake is wrong. Yet we also affirm that the body is good: God made it, Christ took it, and the Spirit dwells in it (1 Cor 6:19). It is not strange that a person overwhelmed by the Father’s love might weep, laugh, tremble or need to lie down. Edwards put this down to the natural union of soul and body. Our test is Christ: does the experience leave people more in love with Jesus, more humble, more kind, more whole? If so, give thanks. If it leaves them proud, exhausted, confused or controlled, set it aside gently."
        ]
      },
      {
        title: "What to do",
        body: [
          "Do not seek manifestations; seek Jesus. If your body responds to God, you do not need to be ashamed, and you do not need to make it bigger. If it does not, you are not missing out. Quiet stillness is also a bodily response to God (Ps 46:10).",
          "Keep your freedom. You can always choose to sit, stand, step back or leave. A healthy ministry will never push you over, mock you for not falling, or tell you to ‘let go’ of your self-control. If there is pressure, that is a signal to step away.",
          "Go gently if you have a history of trauma, seizures, dissociation, panic or psychosis; intense meetings can affect your body and mind strongly. Talk with a trusted leader and, if needed, a medical or mental-health professional. And always look at the fruit over weeks and months, not minutes: love, joy, peace, patience and kindness are the Spirit’s true manifestations (Gal 5:22-23)."
        ]
      }
    ],
    refs: ["Gen 17:3", "2 Chr 5:13-14", "Jer 23:9", "Dan 10:8-11", "Ezek 1:28", "Rev 1:17", "Isa 66:2", "Acts 4:31", "Acts 2:13-15", "Eph 5:18-21", "John 18:6", "Mark 9:20-26", "1 Cor 14:40", "1 Cor 6:19", "Ps 46:10", "Gal 5:22-23"],
    evidence: [
      { text: "“A work is not to be judged of by any effects on the bodies of men; such as tears, trembling, groans, loud outcries, agonies of body, or the failing of bodily strength.” “We cannot conclude that persons are under the influence of the true Spirit because we see such effects upon their bodies... nor on the other hand, have we any reason to conclude, from any such outward appearances, that persons are not under the influence of the Spirit of God.”", source: "Jonathan Edwards, The Distinguishing Marks (1741), as quoted in Sam Storms, “Edwards on Revival – Part II”", url: "https://www.samstorms.org/all-articles/post/edwards-on-revival---part-ii" },
      { text: "“Estimates of attendance ran between 10,000 and 25,000; estimates of the slain from 1,000 to 3,000.” “Though only a minority fell, some parts of the grounds were strewn like a battlefield.” Some ministers, like John Lyle, “believed it wrong for preachers to coax such emotionalism by hysterical preaching.”", source: "Mark Galli, “Revival at Cane Ridge”, Christian History 45 (1995)", url: "https://christianhistoryinstitute.org/magazine/article/revival-at-cane-ridge" },
      { text: "The Toronto Blessing “began in the storefront facility of the Toronto Airport Vineyard Fellowship in January 1994, when participants in revival services manifested intense physical responses to prayer—crying, twitching, shaking, uncontrollable laughter, and falling to the floor.” “In Dec. 1995... John Wimber, released the Airport Vineyard from the Vineyard denomination for reasons of growing unhappiness with the emphasis on the extraordinary manifestations of the Spirit.”", source: "Encyclopedia.com, “Toronto Blessing” (Edith L. Blumhofer, Contemporary American Religion; and The Concise Oxford Dictionary of World Religions)", url: "https://www.encyclopedia.com/philosophy-and-religion/christianity/christianity-general/toronto-blessing" },
      { text: "<em>Ekstasis</em>: “a displacement of the mind, i.e. bewilderment, ‘ecstasy’”. Seven New Testament occurrences: Mark 5:42; 16:8; Luke 5:26; Acts 3:10 (amazement) and Acts 10:10; 11:5; 22:17 (trance).", source: "Bible Hub, Strong’s Greek 1611, ἔκστασις (ekstasis)", url: "https://biblehub.com/greek/1611.htm" }
    ]
  }
